TR=[{"n":"Traditional lambic and gueuze","g":"Food & drink","r":"Pajottenland and Senne valley, south-west of Brussels (Belgium)","p":"13th century to present; gueuze EU TSG 1997/98",
 "d":"Spontaneously fermented wheat beer, aged in wood and blended into gueuze or refermented on fruit, guarded by the High Council for Artisanal Lambic Beers (HORAL).",
 "tech":"Wort cooled overnight in an open coolship, inoculated by airborne yeasts and bacteria, brewed October to May, aged in barrels with old dry hops, blended young with old",
 "mat":"Barley malt, unmalted wheat, aged hops, oak barrels, whole fruit (Schaarbeek cherries, raspberries)",
 "forms":["Lambic","Gueuze","Fruit lambic"],"ctr":["Cantillon (Anderlecht)","3 Fonteinen, Oud Beersel (Beersel)","Boon (Lembeek)","Lindemans (Vlezenbeek)","Girardin (Sint-Ulriks-Kapelle)","Hanssens (Dworp)","De Cam (Gooik)","Tilquin (Rebecq)"],
 "auth":"'Oude Gueuze'/'Oude Geuze' and 'Oude Kriek' on the label mean unsweetened blends of 100% lambic; HORAL contested 'Méthode Gueuze' abroad (Jester King now uses 'Méthode Traditionnelle').","s":W+"Gueuze"}]
out={"kinds":K,"brands":BR,"traditions":TR,"notes":"All eight planned kinds are new (no old data). Archetypes per brief: Cantillon, 3 Fonteinen, Boon, Oud Beersel across lambic/gueuze/fruit lambic; Rodenbach Grand Cru; Liefmans Goudenband; Berliner Kindl Weisse; Leipziger Gose (maker-free; HTML source for Bayerischer Bahnhof unavailable, so BJCP 23 cited); Russian River and Jester King. HORAL as tradition entry cited to the Gueuze article (horal.be returned 503). Left out: Ritterguts/Bayerischer Bahnhof brand entries and founding dates (no retrieved page), Boon framboise and Mariage Parfait year, Timmermans/Mort Subite/Belle-Vue brand entries (versions only), Allagash Coolship and other US spontaneous beers (not on retrieved pages). Faro kept as archetype under Lambic rather than its own kind."}
